# Code review — five axes

Adapted from https://github.com/addyosmani/agent-skills (code-review-and-quality).

Review every change across five dimensions:

1. **Correctness** — matches the spec/PRD; edge cases (null/empty/boundary) and error paths
   handled, not just happy path; tests actually test the right thing; no off-by-one/races.
2. **Readability & simplicity** — descriptive names; straightforward control flow; could it be
   fewer lines? abstractions earn their complexity (don't generalize before the 3rd use); no
   dead code / no-op shims.
3. **Architecture** — fits existing patterns; clean module boundaries; no duplication that
   should be shared (esp. metric logic → reuse `core/`); dependencies flow the right way.
4. **Security** — input validated; secrets out of code/logs/VCS; authz checked; see `security`.
5. **Performance** — no needless work in hot paths; queries bounded + indexed; see `performance`.

**Approval standard:** approve when the change definitely improves overall code health and
follows conventions, even if imperfect. Don't block over style. Pair with `reviewer` for the
project-specific red-flag checklist.
